TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42991162
TI  - Sympathovagal Imbalance and Insulin Resistance: The Autonomic Nervous System as a Convergent Therapeutic Target for Breaking a Bidirectional Pathogenic Cycle.
AU  - Mohr Jan Alexander
AU  - Jiravsky Otakar
AU  - Rucki Lucjan
AU  - Jiravska Godula Bogna
AU  - Samiec Jan
AU  - Pekarova Anna
AU  - Jakubovicova Eva
AU  - Chovancik Jan
AU  - Novakova Marie
AU  - Pekar Matej
JO  - Diabetes, metabolic syndrome and obesity : targets and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2147/DMSO.S604281
AB  - The autonomic nervous system (ANS) and insulin resistance (IR) are linked through a bidirectional pathogenic cycle established during the normoglycaemic, hyperinsulinaemic phase of metabolic disease, years before conventional glucose thresholds are crossed. This review identifies the ANS and IR as convergent therapeutic targets for breaking this vicious cycle, synthesising evidence from autonomic physiology, clinical diabetology, and neuroscience into an integrated mechanistic framework. Two mechanistically distinct arms sustain the cycle: a fast, potentially reversible functional arm driven by hyperinsulinaemia - mediated sympathoexcitation via hypothalamic insulin-signalling pathways, and a slow, progressively irreversible structural arm driven by hyperglycaemia-induced autonomic neuropathy. Experimental evidence demonstrates that inducible peripheral sympathetic blockade prevents overnutrition-induced insulin resistance, establishing sympathetic overactivity as a primary causal driver rather than a consequence. Cross-sectional data have identified cardiac autonomic dysfunction in normoglycaemic individuals with elevated 1-hour post-load glucose who meet no pre-diabetes definition, revealing a population undetectable by current screening criteria. Prospective data from a landmark randomised prevention trial confirmed that autonomic improvement independently predicts diabetes prevention beyond weight loss, with hazard ratios of 0.84-0.85 for incident diabetes. Cardiac autonomic neuropathy, detectable via heart rate variability analysis, is present in 9-39% of pre-diabetic populations and confers a pooled relative mortality risk of 2.14 across 15 studies. Therapeutic interventions targeting both autonomic rebalancing and insulin sensitisation - including structured exercise, bariatric surgery, vagal nerve stimulation, renal denervation, and metabolic pharmacotherapy - converge on identifiable nodes of this feedback loop, enabling stage-matched targeting. We propose a risk stratification framework integrating autonomic assessment with glycaemic staging, including a proposed "Stage 0" category for normoglycaemic individuals with elevated 1-hour post-load glucose, to guide early, targeted intervention when the cycle remains breakable.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42991162/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42983209
TI  - Antidiabetic Therapy and Atrial Fibrillation: Pathophysiological Links, Therapeutic Implications, and Evidence-Based Outcomes.
AU  - Zisis Marios
AU  - Cheiletzaris Theofanis
AU  - Pieper Orestis Filippos
AU  - Kotsovelou Vasiliki
AU  - Spartalis Kyriakos
AU  - Pechlivanidis Sotiris
AU  - Mystakidi Vasiliki Chara
AU  - Katsarou Ourania
AU  - Gialafos Elias
AU  - Theofilis Panagiotis
AU  - Oikonomou Evangelos
AU  - Siasos Gerasimos
JO  - Current cardiology reviews
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2174/011573403X487530260806114153
AB  - Diabetes Mellitus (DM) is strongly associated with an increased risk, severity, and recurrence of Atrial Fibrillation (AF). This narrative review aims to synthesize the mechanistic links between DM and AF and evaluate the impact of major antidiabetic drug categories on AF incidence, burden, and post-ablation outcomes. A focused narrative review of the literature was conducted using predefined eligibility criteria for study selection and specific keywords to search major scientific databases and grey literature. Hyperglycemia, oxidative stress, epicardial adipose tissue expansion, autonomic imbalance, and atrial remodeling represent core pathophysiological mechanisms connecting DM and AF. Among antidiabetic pharmacotherapies, the glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist semaglutide displays a potential to reduce AF incidence. Sodium-glucose transporter 2 inhibitors have similarly been shown to lower the incidence of AF in most of the available evidence, while offering an emerging, preliminary benefit for post-ablation success. Metformin also demonstrates a positive impact across both primary and secondary AF prevention, albeit with less robust evidence. Other incretin therapies, including tirzepatide, exhibit neutral effects, while thiazolidinediones and dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors yield conflicting results. Data from insulin and sulfonylureas suggest an unfavorable risk profile, though potentially confounded by indication. Antidiabetic therapy selection may have clinically meaningful implications for AF risk modification and rhythm-control outcomes. Weight-centric and cardio-protective strategies, particularly SGLT2 inhibitors and semaglutide, alongside the traditional option of metformin, show promising signals. Nevertheless, available data derive largely from unadjudicated adverseevent reporting and observational cohorts, currently precluding definitive class-wide or agentspecific recommendations. Incorporating atrial fibrillation risk into the individualization of antidiabetic therapy is a promising and clinically relevant strategy. However, establishing any specific agent as a preferred option for AF management remains premature in the absence of dedicated trials with primary AF endpoints.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42983209/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42959996
TI  - Treatment-Induced Neuropathy Presenting as Thoracic Radiculopathy and Abdominal Wall Protrusion After Marked Glycemic Improvement With Tirzepatide.
AU  - Bagheri Kian
AU  - Buck Erin
AU  - Muley Suraj
JO  - Cureus
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.7759/cureus.117153
AB  - Treatment-induced neuropathy of diabetes (TIND) is an uncommon acute neuropathic complication associated with marked improvement in glycemic control. It typically presents with severe neuropathic pain and may include autonomic dysfunction, although focal presentations have also been described. We report the case of a 67-year-old man with long-standing type 2 diabetes mellitus who developed severe thoracoabdominal neuropathic pain and abdominal wall protrusion following marked improvement in glycemic control after the initiation of tirzepatide. Hemoglobin A1c decreased from 11.0% to 6.6% over seven months, accompanied by a 17-pound weight loss. Several weeks after this metabolic improvement, he developed burning, stabbing, and electric shock-like thoracoabdominal pain with dysesthesia, hyperesthesia, and abdominal wall weakness. Extensive gastrointestinal, surgical, and structural evaluations were unrevealing. Electrodiagnostic testing demonstrated a generalized sensorimotor polyneuropathy, but needle electromyography of the thoracic or abdominal musculature was not performed. The clinical presentation was consistent with thoracic radiculoneuropathy occurring in the setting of marked glycemic improvement, although TIND could not be definitively established. This case highlights a focal truncal presentation temporally associated with glycemic improvement following tirzepatide therapy and emphasizes the diagnostic challenges in distinguishing TIND from spontaneous diabetic thoracic radiculoneuropathy.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42959996/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42930907
TI  - Cardiometabolic Substrate Modification in Atrial Fibrillation: Obesity and Epicardial Adipose Tissue in the Era of Incretin-Based Therapies and Catheter Ablation.
AU  - Matteucci Andrea
AU  - Bonanni Michela
AU  - Sgarra Luca
AU  - Nesti Lorenzo
AU  - Mariani Marco Valerio
AU  - Pierucci Nicola
AU  - Cersosimo Angelica
AU  - Pittorru Raimondo
AU  - Nardi Federico
AU  - Colivicchi Furio
JO  - Journal of cardiovascular development and disease
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3390/jcdd13100512
AB  - Atrial fibrillation (AF) is increasingly understood as the electrical expression of a systemic and atrial cardiometabolic disease rather than an isolated disorder of pulmonary-vein triggers. Obesity, insulin resistance, hypertension, sleep-disordered breathing, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, and visceral adiposity promote atrial stretch, inflammation, fibrosis, autonomic imbalance, and conduction heterogeneity. Epicardial adipose tissue (EAT), particularly the peri-left-atrial depot, may provide a local mechanistic bridge between systemic metabolic dysfunction and atrial cardiomyopathy through paracrine, inflammatory, fibrotic, and infiltrative pathways. This framework has direct therapeutic implications. Structured weight loss, cardiorespiratory fitness, alcohol reduction, and comprehensive risk-factor management can reduce AF burden and improve rhythm-control outcomes, although the magnitude of benefit varies across populations and trial designs. Incretin-based therapies have introduced a potentially important pharmacologic route to substrate modification. Semaglutide and tirzepatide produce substantial weight loss and favorable cardiovascular and heart-failure effects, while emerging observational and prospective data suggest lower AF incidence and post-ablation recurrence. However, evidence for reduced post-ablation recurrence remains predominantly non-randomized and is susceptible to residual confounding, treatment-selection effects, and healthy-user bias. Causality, weight-independent antiarrhythmic effects, optimal timing relative to ablation, and durability after treatment withdrawal remain unresolved. Observational sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitor data support further investigation of cardiometabolic approaches to rhythm control, although randomized evidence has not consistently demonstrated benefit. Our review integrates mechanistic, imaging, lifestyle, pharmacologic, and electrophysiological evidence and proposes a practical model in which catheter ablation and cardiometabolic substrate modification are complementary strategies.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42930907/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42922826
TI  - GLP-1/GIP Uptake, Indication, and Access Pathways Among US Adults in the Understanding America Study.
AU  - Chaturvedi Ritika
AU  - Gracner Tadeja
AU  - Perez-Arce Francisco
AU  - Suen Sze-Chuan
AU  - Jin Jing
AU  - Orriens Bart
AU  - Pacula Rosalie Liccardo
AU  - Ward Alison Sexton
AU  - Haile Rebecca
AU  - Kapteyn Arie
JO  - medRxiv : the preprint server for health sciences
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.64898/2026.08.28.26361368
AB  - Evidence on GLP-1/GIP therapies is largely derived from trials enrolling selected populations or medical records that miss utilization outside healthcare channels. No nationally representative cohort has characterized real-world uptake, indications, and access. To characterize GLP-1/GIP prevalence, indication, clinical profile, and access. Prospective cohort study with three GLP-1/GIP surveillance waves (March 2024, December 2024, October 2025). The Understanding America Study, an address-based, nationally representative panel of approximately 15,000 US adults aged 18+ years initiated in 2014. UAS participants responding to at least one surveillance wave (n=9150). GLP-1/GIP use status (never vs any use, comprising current and former use), self-reported primary indication (diabetes, weight loss, or other), and access pathway (traditional vs non-traditional). Survey-weighted prevalence of GLP-1/GIP use, overall and by indication and access pathway; sociodemographic, cardiometabolic, treatment, and access characteristics; and smartwatch-derived resting heart rate, heart rate variability, maximum activity heart rate, step count, and sleep duration and variability. Among n=9150 adults (1274 with any use; 60.9% female; median age 53 years), weighted prevalence increased 46%, from 8.2% (March 2024) to 12.0% (October 2025) representing 32 million. Weight-loss indications grew, reaching nearly half of use (4.1% to 5.6%); diabetes-indicated use was stable (5.3% to 5.4%). Users carried high cardiometabolic burden (obesity, 68.2%; diabetes, 53.6%) but diverged by indication: diabetes-indicated users were older (median, 59 vs 49 years), whereas weight-loss-indicated users were more often female (69.9% vs 51.3%) and healthier. One in three users (~9 million) had non-traditional access, especially in weight-loss-indicated users, of whom 33% had no conventional prescription; 41% used compounding, online, or foreign pharmacies; and, 43% lacked coverage. Non-traditional users were five times as likely to report an unlisted, likely compounded formulation (19.8% vs 4.1%). All p<0.05. Real-world GLP-1/GIP use has grown rapidly and diversified substantially in indication, access, and population profile. One in 3 users obtained treatment through nontraditional channels largely invisible to claims data, raising long-term safety, efficacy, and coverage questions. GLIMMER provides a public, nationally representative longitudinal evidence base for future payer and provider decisions.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42922826/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42903342
TI  - Molecular signaling networks that connect tissue dysfunction to systemic insulin resistance.
AU  - Mir Rashid
AU  - Algehainy Naseh A
AU  - Jalal Mohammed M
AU  - Altayar Malik A
AU  - Almotairi Reema M
AU  - Tayeb Faris J
AU  - Bedaiwi Ruqaiah I
AU  - Altemani Faisal H
AU  - Almasoudi Kholoud S
AU  - Altedlawi Albalawi Ibrahim
AU  - Elfaki Imadeldin
JO  - Frontiers in endocrinology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3389/fendo.2026.1952238
AB  - Insulin resistance is conventionally described as a single condition, but it is better understood as the point at which several tissue-specific signaling failures, each following its own molecular logic and its own timeline, converge into one measurable phenotype. This review treats insulin resistance as a network rather than a collection of independent organ pathologies. Because the components of this network rest on very different kinds of evidence, each node and edge is graded explicitly by study design and causal status, and the time-staged sequence is presented as a falsifiable proposal rather than an established progression. Put simply, insulin resistance is treated here not as one defect but as the shared endpoint of several tissue-specific failures that develop at different times and communicate with one another. Liver, skeletal muscle, adipose tissue, pancreatic islets, the gut, and the brain are each examined as nodes with characteristic signaling lesions built from a shared architecture of diacylglycerol- and ceramide-activated kinases, inhibitory serine phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate proteins, and endoplasmic reticulum stress, while inter-organ messengers, incretins, adipokines, bile acids, and autonomic circuits, are treated as the edges that couple these nodes together. The pancreas is included not as a site of resistance but as the network's compensatory arm, and the brain is treated as an integrated hypothalamic-vagal node rather than a separate neurocognitive topic, closing a loop in which adipose-derived signals impair central insulin and leptin action, weakened central signaling relaxes hepatic glucose control, and reduced satiety sustains the intake that drove adipose dysfunction in the first place. A dedicated section addresses the post-translational and organelle-level machinery, shared serine kinases, ubiquitin-mediated IRS degradation, O-GlcNAcylation, and mitochondria-endoplasmic reticulum contact sites, that allows the same small set of molecular tools to produce different tissue-specific outputs. These mechanisms are then placed on an explicit time axis moving from silent compensation, in which adipose and hypothalamic changes precede any measurable defect elsewhere, through early decompensation in liver and muscle, to established decompensation marked by β-cell failure and self-sustaining feed-forward loops, a staging distinction that is largely absent from mechanistic studies conducted at a single, typically late, disease stage. This framing helps explain a pattern in current therapeutics: interventions that act on several network nodes at once, most notably incretin-based therapies, have outperformed mechanistically well-motivated single-node strategies, including direct inhibition of shared convergence kinases. The review closes by identifying where the network remains least understood, principally in longitudinal, tissue-resolved human data capable of testing the proposed staging sequence directly.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42903342/
ER  - 

